00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:22.139
When Tommy and Carrie walk into the prom,
the band is playing a song whose lyrics sing,

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:23.559
“The Devil’s Got Ahold of his Soul.”

00:06:23.559 --> 00:06:27.639
The song is called Education Blues by Vance
or Towers, and if you flip the gender of the

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:31.060
subject of this tune, it could easily be interpreted
to be about Carrie White.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.060
Let’s roll it.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:37.880
“As I look back upon my younger daze, I
really was a maniac.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:43.380
Blowing up a john with a cherry bomb, Gave
the janitor a cardiac.”

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:48.000
It’s talking about a school aged kid who
doesn’t fit into societal norms and subsequently

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.360
goes on 
a rampage.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:54.860
“The teachers would say, ‘They should
lock him away And throw away all of the keys’

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:55.560
-- please!”

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.180
In Carrie’s case she has a reputation around
the school, causing constant bullying and

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.460
graffiti reading “Carrie White burn in hell.”

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:07.020
“He's outta control, The devil's gotta hold
of his soul!"

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.060
Obviously a reference to Carrie’s mom believing
that she’s an agent of the devil.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:15.900
Also uncoincidental I’m sure, is Tommy Ross’s
reasoning behind voting for themselves for Prom

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:16.840
King and Queen.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.340
“Come on. To the devil with false modesty.”

00:07:20.349 --> 00:07:23.909
The prom itself is a very foreign thing to
Carrie, and I don’t think it would be too

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:28.169
much of a stretch to call an event centered
around social interaction to be very alien

00:07:28.169 --> 00:07:29.169
for her.

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:30.169
Not you.

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:31.169
We’ll cover you one day.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:35.870
But the prom scene is filmed as if it truly
is another planet from the theme, Love Among

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:39.100
The Stars, to Carrie describing her experience
to Ms. Collins,

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.719
“It's like being on Mars.”

00:07:40.719 --> 00:07:44.929
to the shot of Carrie and Tommy dancing where
they spin on an axis as the camera orbits

00:07:44.929 --> 00:07:47.059
around them, much like a solar system.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:50.160
Also, we’re not gonna ignore the fact that
there are clearly Dragon Balls on stage,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:50.660
right?

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.820
Vegeta, how many Things You Missed in Carrie?

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:54.479
“It’s over 9”

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.689
But before we look any further into the prom
scene, let’s discuss how it was setup.

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:01.840
When Carrie is sitting in English class there
are a couple of things to note, the first

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.159
of which is poster for Hamlet, the Shakesperian
tragedy known for… spoiler alert even though

00:08:06.159 --> 00:08:10.770
it came out 420 years ago... the fact that
nearly everyone dies in the end.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:15.129
The shot with both Carrie and Tommy in frame
utilizes something called a split focus diopter.

00:08:15.129 --> 00:08:16.659
Let me explain.

00:08:16.659 --> 00:08:20.889
The foreground and background are sharp and
in focus, but the middle ground is not.

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:24.640
Normally, this effect would be impossible
to achieve because in cinematography, you

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:29.929
can only have your focal length set to one
position at a time, just as under the normal circumstances

00:08:29.929 --> 00:08:33.370
of high school culture, Tommy and Carrie would
never go out on a date.

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:38.510
But director Brian DiPalma uses this device,
the split focus diopter to set different focal

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:42.840
lengths for the left and right side of the
image, just like how under extraordinary circumstances,

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.270
Tommy and Carrie are together.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:47.750
You can see it in some other DiPalma films,
but it’s honestly a bit dated, today if

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.660
we wanted to achieve that, we’d probably
use a greenscreen.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:52.440
Not that I’d know anything about that.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.820
The name of the high school has been changed
from Ewen High School in the book to Bates

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:56.840
High School in the film.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:00.530
I think this was an homage to the classic
horror film, maybe the most classic of all

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:03.960
horror films, Psycho, which takes place at
the Bates Motel.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.140
The music that accompanies Carrie’s use
of her powers…

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.980
Is very similar to the score from Psycho.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.620
[clip]

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.570
And of course, both movies feature iconic shower
scenes.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:21.100
The mascot of Bates High School is the Stingers,
a hornet like creature.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:24.620
In film music terminology, a stinger is a
short sound used for emphasis,

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:25.620
"EMPHASIS!"

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:26.270
much like the

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.530
Psycho inspired sound I just mentioned.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.660
That might be why Bates’ mascot is the Stingers.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:34.590
The idea comes back during Carrie’s meltdown
at the prom where she sees in kaleidoscope

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:38.150
vision, something commonly associated with
how a hornet might see the world.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:42.070
In this scene, Carrie is essentially an angry
hornet whose classmates have just shaken up

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:43.070
her nest.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:46.000
There are a couple easter eggs referencing
obscurities from the book.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.160
One of the nominees for Prom Queen is Cora
Wilson, who we can assume is one of the Wilson

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.580
sisters mentioned earlier by the coach.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.320
"Watson? And the Wilsons?"

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:59.140
The Wilson Sisters are assumed to be adapted
from the Thibodeau sisters in the novel, a

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.820
pair of twins who partake in the bullying
of Carrie.

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:06.150
In the movie, Cora is seen dancing with this
guy, who may or may not be her date, Frank

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:09.140
Green but is definitely credited as “The
Beak.”

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.230
Now in the novel, The Beak is a boy named Freddy
Holt.

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:14.470
He’s only mentioned once; he’s the first
name that pops into the principal’s head

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.110
when he hears that Carrie White is going to
prom.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:21.070
The novel says he’s a misfit for his huge
nose and small frame, but the movie gives

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:23.220
him his own awkward charm.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.100
"Wait a second, I know just the tuxedo for him."

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:28.540
"Well what do you think, huh?"

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:31.680
"I like it. I think it's terrific."

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.680
"Look at that. No ruffles at all."

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.940
The other Wilson sister is Mary Lila Grace,
who is also one of the nominees for Prom Queen,

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:41.860
although her date doesn’t seem to have a
counterpart in the book as far as I can tell.

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:44.780
When Billy and Chris are in the car together,
he momentarily calls her “Chucky”,

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:47.460
"What are you scared Chucky?"

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:48.180
a nickname

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:51.190
he uses a couple of times to get under her
skin in the novel.

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:54.380
Following the prom scene, there is one more
event that takes place.

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:58.840
Sue Snell is one of the only survivors after
losing her boyfriend and most of her classmates.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:02.230
We see her come up to the destroyed remains
of the house, the soft bloom effect on the

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:05.590
camera is very similar to the scene where
Tommy pulls up to Carrie’s house to ask

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:09.820
her to the prom again, it’s very dream-like,
but this dream of Sue’s will soon become

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:10.820
a nightmare.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:15.260
Sue brings her a bouquet of flowers as beautiful
woodwind music plays… but wait… these

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:18.070
are red flowers, the color of the devil.

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:19.120
Something is not right here.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:23.470
The cars in the background are driving backwards,
one of which may be the very same car that

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:25.580
that Billy and Chris use to try to run over Carrie.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.920
The religious symbols come back for one final
appearance.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:32.780
The for sale sign is in the shape of a cross
with vandalism reading “Carrie White Burns

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:36.370
in Hell”, pointing down to the rubble, the
last remains of the house.

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.660
When Sue steps onto this rubble, something
changes.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:40.920
She has entered hell.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.360
The scene becomes even more unnerving to the
subconscious as the sky turns from blue to

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:49.120
black before the final horror takes place.

